    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Chromosomes are segregated equally to each daughter cell during mitosis. A protein complex known as the kinetochore is responsible for linking chromosomes to spindle microtubules during mitosis. Mitotic assembly of the kinetochore requires a\r\nspecialized underlying region of chromatin called the centromere. Centromeres are epigenetically marked by the incorporation of the histone H3 variant CENP-A, which recruits the 16-subunit constitutive centromere-associated network (CCAN). The\r\norganization of the CCAN, as well as how the CCAN recruits the kinetochore to promote proper chromosome segregation, remains unclear. Previous work has demonstrated a fundamental role for the CCAN protein CENP-C in recruiting the rest of the CCAN and the kinetochore protein Mis12. A detailed understanding of the role of CENP-C has been hampered by the inability to rapidly and efficiently degrade endogenous CENP-C. Here we have characterized a cell line expressing a selectively degradable form of CENP-C, allowing us to efficiently deplete endogenous CENP-C in as little as 45 minutes. Depletion of CENP-C did not result in defects in the localization of CENP-T and CENP-K shortly after depletion, but did result in defects after 72 hours. These results suggest that CENP-C may play separable roles in CCAN recruitment and maintenance, and they\r\nhighlight how fine temporal manipulation of protein levels can help us dissect these mechanisms.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
